<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Franconia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Franconia is a historical region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> centred on the valley of the Main, now mostly in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> with parts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>. One of the stem duchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a>, it was dominated in the early 10th century by the Conradines, whose leader became King Conrad I. Later divided among prince-bishoprics, Hohenzollern margraves, counts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city">free cities</a>, it formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconian-circle">Franconian Circle</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/franconia.jpg" alt="Franconia" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mikmaq · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franconia_(Germany).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Franconia is not a political entity with a fixed area, and it is debated whether some districts belong to it. It is defined by its history, its culture and especially the East Franconian dialect, and in part by the territory of the former Duchy of Franconia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconian-circle">Franconian Circle</a>. Most of it lies north and south of the River Main and its tributary the Regnitz, in the three Bavarian districts of Lower, Middle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-franconia">Upper Franconia</a>. Upland ranges such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconian-jura">Franconian Jura</a>, the Fichtel Mountains, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhon">Rhön</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spessart">Spessart</a> surround it. Its highest mountain is the Schneeberg in the Fichtel Mountains, at 1,051 metres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Fossil finds show people were living in the region about 600,000 years ago. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, from about 800 BCE, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> were the first people discernible there, and they built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-fort">hill forts</a> and settlements such as the one Ptolemy called oppidum Menosgada on the Staffelberg. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> expanded in the first century BCE and Germanic tribes advanced from the north, Celtic culture declined. The southern parts came under Roman control, but most of the region stayed in free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> gained the region through victories over the Alamanni and Thuringians in the 6th century. Its name does not mean it was the homeland of the Franks, since it was only partly settled by Franks from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Frankish Empire</a> was divided, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a> was formed from the territories of the dioceses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, Worms, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> and Speyer, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a> was added later. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> began to settle the north-east from the 7th century. Irish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> monks first spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> strongly there, and Saint Kilian was martyred at Würzburg in the late 7th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Saint Boniface</a> probably carried the mission to the ordinary people.</p><p>On 2 July 1500, under Emperor Maximilian I, the Empire was divided into Imperial Circles, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconian-circle">Franconian Circle</a> was formed in 1512. The region was severely affected by Kleinstaaterei, a patchwork of tiny states, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> used the nominal title of Duke of Franconia. In 1559 the circle was given jurisdiction over coinage. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> ended, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s restructuring of the south German states gave most of Franconia to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Franconia is divided among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, and its largest part belongs to Bavaria. Its largest city is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/furth">Fürth</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erlangen">Erlangen</a>, and the region has roughly five million inhabitants. Franconians count as one of the four tribes of Bavaria. Large areas of Middle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-franconia">Upper Franconia</a> are Protestant, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-franconia">Lower Franconia</a> and western Upper Franconia are mostly Catholic, reflecting the territories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconian-circle">Franconian Circle</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Franconia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the Imperial Recess (Reichsdeputationshauptschluss) of 1803 the right-bank lands, including Heidelberg and Mannheim, went mainly to Baden, with smaller parts to Hesse-Darmstadt, the princes of Leiningen and others, while Elector Maximilian IV Joseph was compensated in <b>Franconia</b> and Swabia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The name of the Franks survives in France, in the German region of <b>Franconia</b> and in the city of Frankfurt, the ‘ford of the Franks’.</blockquote><a href="/empire/franks">Franks · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The result was a kingdom of about 76,000 square kilometres, the second largest German state, joining Catholic Old Bavaria with largely Protestant <b>Franconia</b> and the Palatinate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The name of the Franks lives on in France and <b>Franconia</b>, and later rulers of both France and the Holy Roman Empire claimed Charlemagne’s inheritance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>When Louis the Child, the last Carolingian ruler of East Francia, died in 911, the dukes and magnates elected Conrad, Duke of <b>Franconia</b>, as their king, and in 919 chose the Saxon duke Henry the Fowler.</blockquote><a href="/government/elective-monarchy">Elective monarchy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>An invading Hungarian army won a crushing victory at Eisenach over an East Frankish army of troops from <b>Franconia</b>, Saxony and Thuringia, led by Duke Burchard of Thuringia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-3">3 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
